  faqs: [
    { question: "What are my options if Yawal has no IB or IGCSE school?", answer: "The simplest is to keep your child in the present school and add an online tutor for the target syllabus. The other routes are sitting IGCSE as a private candidate through an exam centre, or moving to a school in Indore, Surat or Pune, where confirmed campuses exist. Most families begin with tutoring because it keeps every later option open." },
    { question: "Will anyone from IB Gram come to our home in Yawal?", answer: "No. Home visits are available only in Gurugram and parts of Delhi NCR, so nobody visits Yawal. Your child instead takes online home tuition: a live lesson on a laptop from home, with the tutor on video and a whiteboard both can write on. It is private, one-to-one teaching, just not in person." },
    { question: "What information do you need before suggesting a tutor?", answer: "The programme or board, the subject and level, your child's present standing, the exam session in view and the hours your household can protect. With those we search nationally, since Yawal has no pool of IB teachers, for someone who has taught that exact course lately, then propose one with a short reason and a free first lesson." },
    { question: "Is IGCSE possible from Yawal if my child stays in the present school?", answer: "Usually yes, by entering as a private candidate. Pick Cambridge or Pearson Edexcel, fix the syllabus code and tier, then ask an exam centre, likely in Jalgaon, Nashik, Pune or Mumbai, whether it accepts private entries and by which date. The lessons run online and leave the school timetable alone. Ask early, because places and deadlines shift." },
    { question: "How is the tuition fee worked out?", answer: "It reflects the programme and level, the length and number of sessions, and how recently the tutor has taught your exact course, and there is no published rate card. You receive the figure in writing before booking. The student's address plays no part, because nobody travels, and the free first lesson lets you judge the teaching before discussing money." },
    { question: "What does my child do in the free lesson?", answer: "They work through a real topic from their own syllabus with the proposed tutor on video. The tutor asks questions to locate strengths and gaps and explains a concept or two. Afterwards you receive a plan for the first month and the rate in writing, and decide whether to continue, change the plan or meet someone else." },
    { question: "How far can a tutor help with an IA, the Extended Essay or TOK?", answer: "A tutor can coach the process: unpack the criteria, help narrow a question, discuss how evidence might be gathered and comment candidly on drafts. Writing, rewriting or supplying any assessed content is ruled out, because it breaches IB academic-integrity rules and puts the qualification at risk. The submitted work has to be the student's own." },
    { question: "My child has studied in Marathi medium. How should we prepare for IB or IGCSE English?", answer: "Start the written English early and keep it light but regular. A glossary of core terms, one short written explanation each week and marking against the real scheme build fluency steadily. Students who read Marathi well can also take Marathi A, and Hindi B is widely available, which banks a strong result while English catches up." },
    { question: "Which IB courses are we likely to find tutors for?", answer: "Most of them: Maths AA and AI, Physics, Chemistry and Biology, Economics, Business Management, Computer Science, Geography, ESS, History, English A, Hindi B and TOK. Marathi A is checked when we read your brief. Send the subject, HL or SL and the session, and we will tell you plainly whether a suitable specialist is free." },
    { question: "Is there anything for younger children, in PYP or MYP?", answer: "Yes. For PYP, lessons build reading, writing and number sense alongside the inquiry habits behind the Exhibition, in short weekly sessions. For MYP, they cover criterion-based work in sciences, maths and language and literature, plus the Personal Project journal. Where no IB school is near, a tutor helps a transferred child keep the routines that school would have supplied." },
    { question: "How do we manage power cuts and weak signal in the taluka?", answer: "Tell the tutor as soon as a lesson is affected and rejoin when you can; a mobile hotspot is a useful backup. A lost half hour can usually be recovered by moving another session within the week. Summer heat and monsoon days are the likeliest causes, so finish drafts and mock papers well before any deadline." },
    { question: "How far ahead of the exam should tutoring start?", answer: "As early as the course allows: Class 11 for the Diploma, Grade 9 for IGCSE. A full two years lets written English, command words and the first assessment plan settle calmly. Six months still helps, with the tutor working on the topics worth most marks and on timed papers before the target series." },
    { question: "Can tuition run beside MHT-CET or NEET preparation?", answer: "Many students manage both, with a realistic timetable. The subject content overlaps, but entrance papers and IB or IGCSE papers are written in different styles, so each needs its own practice. Our tutors cover the IB or IGCSE side, and entrance coaching is separate. Read each exam's latest eligibility rules for your board before committing." },
    { question: "Cambridge or Edexcel: how do we choose?", answer: "Choose by practicalities, not reputation. Both are recognised worldwide. Look at which board's exam centre, dates and subject list suit your household, and at any school you may join later. Cambridge sits in May-June and October-November, Edexcel in January and May-June. Once you choose, keep every past paper and mark scheme from that board." },
    { question: "Will a Maharashtra college or university accept an IB or IGCSE record?", answer: "Generally yes, after the right paperwork. The Association of Indian Universities issues equivalence for the IB Diploma and IGCSE-based qualifications, CUET-UG admits students from every board to central universities, and MHT-CET, JEE and NEET each set their own conditions. Start the equivalence paperwork early in Class 12 and check every institution's rules directly." },
    { question: "Will lessons fit around the banana season and festivals?", answer: "Yes. Most families fix one weekday evening and one weekend slot, then agree with the tutor which loading weeks, Akshaya Tritiya, Pola, Ganeshotsav, Diwali or Eid days to skip or shift. Online delivery makes an extra session before a mock easy to add, and no one has to travel to catch up." },
    { question: "What if the tutor is not right for my child?", answer: "Say so and we will suggest another. Progress is reviewed every few weeks, and a change of tutor is a normal part of how the service works. The free first lesson is there to expose a poor fit early, and the rate is agreed in writing at the start, so a change brings no surprises." },
    { question: "Does IB Gram have any link with the schools or boards mentioned here?", answer: "No. IB Gram is independent, with no partnership, contract or endorsement from the IB, Cambridge, Pearson or any school, college or university named on this page, and it cannot speak for them. The names are there to show what exists near Yawal. Admission, exam entry and equivalence are decided by those bodies." },
  ],

